diff --git a/drivers/usb-console/PROBES.md b/drivers/usb-console/PROBES.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..b86fa28
--- /dev/null
+++ b/drivers/usb-console/PROBES.md
@@ -0,0 +1,197 @@
+# SM-G9209 early-kernel USB probe
+
+This diagnostic mode runs the actual embedded Linux Image until a selected
+checkpoint, or until the diagnostic exception vector catches a fault. It
+then **ends that kernel run**, restores the saved loader CPU context, and
+reports the snapshot over the existing CDC connection. It never resumes
+the interrupted kernel invocation. It does not install a Linux gadget
+driver or provide a Linux runtime console.
+
+## Commands and checkpoints
+
+Use the first CDC port, identified by USB serial `G9209-UNILOADER` rather
+than an assumed `/dev/ttyACM` number. `probe N` accepts only **1 through 31**:
+
+| Checkpoint | Location |
+| --- | --- |
+| 1 | Kernel entry, after installing the diagnostic exception vector |
+| 2 | Initial identity map built |
+| 3 | `__cpu_setup` returned |
+| 4 | `__enable_mmu` returned; snapshot includes actual SCTLR.M |
+| 5 | High virtual kernel mapping built |
+| 6 | TTBR1 installed and kernel relocation returned |
+| 7 | High virtual `__primary_switched` entry, before task setup |
+| 8 | Initial task, kernel stack and per-CPU offset installed |
+| 9 | BSS clearing returned |
+| 10 | Early FDT mapping returned |
+| 11 | Feature override/finalise_el2 returned, before `start_kernel` |
+| 12 | Actual C `start_kernel` entry |
+| 13 | `setup_machine_fdt` entry |
+| 14 | FDT fixmap mapping returned |
+| 15 | Before FDT scan |
+| 16 | FDT scan succeeded |
+| 17 | Before read-only FDT remap |
+| 18 | Read-only FDT remap returned |
+| 19 | Machine name obtained |
+| 20 | End of `setup_machine_fdt` |
+| 21 | `setup_machine_fdt` returned to `setup_arch` |
+| 22 | `jump_label_init` returned |
+| 23 | Early parameter parsing returned |
+| 24 | Immediately **before** `local_daif_restore`; the legacy report label says "DAIF restored" |
+| 25 | Before `cpu_uninstall_idmap` |
+| 26 | `cpu_uninstall_idmap` returned |
+| 27 | `arm64_memblock_init` returned |
+| 28 | `paging_init` returned |
+| 29 | Device tree unflattened |
+| 30 | `bootmem_init` returned |
+| 31 | PSCI initialization returned, before boot CPU/SMP initialization |
+
+Other commands:
+
+- `status`: report current loader state and controller registers. It does
+  not reprint the waiting prompt.
+- `log`: replay the loader log from its beginning. The retained log is a
+  bounded **128 KiB** linear buffer; it is not the kernel printk ring.
+- `boot`: stop loader USB DMA and perform a normal kernel entry.
+- `reboot`, `reboot recovery`: select the boot mode in PMU INFORM registers,
+  request native PMU SWRESET, and arm a short watchdog fallback.
+- `watchdog arm`, `watchdog off`, `watchdog status`: inspect/control the
+  diagnostic watchdog. `watchdog test` and `watchdog test recovery` are
+  explicit expiry tests; their names alone do not establish test success.
+- `psci version`, `psci reset`: explicit firmware-call diagnostics, guarded
+  by an observed watchdog countdown. Native `reboot` uses PMU SWRESET.
+- `dt reserve el3`, `dt unreserve el3`: mutate only this loader session's
+  expanded FDT for controlled diagnostic A/B tests. The default board DTS
+  now reserves EL3 `0xfe800000 + 0x01800000` and framebuffer
+  `0xe2a00000 + 0x01000000`, both `no-map`. Removing EL3 protection is solely
+  for reproducing the old fault, not a normal boot configuration. A later
+  `boot` in the same session uses the currently mutated FDT too.
+
+## Diagnostic ABI and memory ownership
+
+The board-specific ABI uses the 24-word `dsh_regs` array:
+
+| Slots | Contents |
+| --- | --- |
+| 0..6 | CPU register snapshot: TCR, MAIR, SCTLR, TTBR1, TTBR0, SP, CurrentEL |
+| 8..11 | Exception ESR, FAR, ELR, and handler progress |
+| 12..15 | Physical loader callback, requested checkpoint, last checkpoint, magic `0x44534850524f4245` |
+| 16..18 | Last early page-table allocation PA, allocation count, phase |
+| 20..22 | FDT physical address, mapped virtual address, size |
+| 23 | Recursive exit guard |
+
+Allocation phase 1 means allocated, 2 fixmapped, 3 zeroed, and 4 fixmap
+cleared. These breadcrumbs are cleaned to the PoC at every phase, but
+firmware may still overwrite RAM during reboot. A retained word alone is
+not proof that it belongs to the current build or attempt.
+
+A normal, freshly copied Image has no armed magic, so probe checkpoints
+fall through. No Linux boot argument registers are repurposed. Armed probe
+runs reserve **32 MiB at `0x47000000..0x49000000`** with memblock before
+kernel allocations can reuse the loader image, context, and USB arena.
+This extra loader reservation applies only while the probe magic is armed.
+The diagnostic code does not add loader RAM or USB MMIO to the kernel's
+page tables for executing loader C.
+
+## Return path and supported lifetime
+
+The mode requires EL1 entry with the MMU off and SP_EL1, a 4 KiB/39-bit VA
+Image at physical `0x90000000`, and execution before `free_initmem()`.
+The supported command set currently stops at checkpoint 31; this is not
+a general runtime kernel-to-loader context switch.
+
+The exit routine `dsh_probe_exit`, including its physical trampoline, is
+linked in normal executable **`.text`**. It must not live in `.idmap.text`:
+the latter is placed in `.rodata.text`, which the permanent kernel TTBR1
+mapping deliberately marks non-executable. Physical execution of normal
+Linux idmap routines remains valid, but calling that high VA after
+`paging_init` would fault. The diagnostic exception handler would then
+retry the same unusable exit, hiding the original progress.
+
+On the high-VA exit path, the code first snapshots the **tested** CPU state.
+It then installs reserved TTBR0, invalidates the relevant TLB state,
+restores the initial identity-map T0SZ, and reinstalls
+`init_idmap_pg_dir` before branching to the physical exit address. The
+kernel's real `cpu_uninstall_idmap()` runs unchanged. The initial idmap
+tables are distinct from `init_pg_dir`, which `paging_init()` frees; the
+initial idmap survives until init memory is reclaimed. Low-VA checkpoints
+already executing through the initial idmap do not replace TTBR0 beneath
+their own instructions.
+
+The physical exit cleans the loaded kernel memory range, disables M/C,
+and branches to loader assembly. That assembly restores the loader's
+callee-saved registers, stack, translation/control registers, VBAR,
+SP_EL0 and thread/per-CPU pointer registers. Only then do loader C and
+USB polling resume.
+
+During armed runs, `__primary_switched` moves VBAR to the **high-VA**
+diagnostic vector table in executable `.text` before TTBR0 is removed.
+Keeping a physical VBAR after idmap uninstall would make the diagnostic
+fault handler itself unreachable. Normal boot installs Linux's standard
+vectors.
+
+Each probe recopies the original Image and clears its NOBITS tail before
+entry. The mutated payload is replaced again after reporting, before
+accepting another probe or normal boot. Probe entry exercises the common
+cache-preparation path, with uncached EL1 entry enforced and the USB DMA
+arena explicitly synchronized. USB ownership remains with the loader;
+its polling is suspended while the kernel runs. An enumerated USB device
+does not prove that the loader command loop is still executing.
+
+## Watchdog and evidence limits
+
+The Exynos7420 watchdog is at **`0x10020000`**, with PERIS PCLK gate bit 7.
+The ARM64 Samsung PMU sequence clears ATLAS/APOLLO bits 23/24 in
+`AUTOMATIC_DISABLE_WDT` (`0x105c0408`) and APOLLO bit 24 in
+`MASK_WDT_RESET_REQUEST` (`0x105c040c`). The old ARM32 bit-0 scheme does not
+unmask this reset route.
+
+After copying and cache preparation, probe entry arms count `0xffff`;
+the loader stops the watchdog immediately on return, before recopying.
+The expected maximum period is approximately 32.5 seconds with a 66 MHz
+parent or 89.5 seconds with 24 MHz. The local `usb_udelay` is a conservative
+spin loop, not a calibrated microsecond time source.
+
+Keep these evidence levels separate:
+
+1. Observing WTCNT decrease establishes that the watchdog clock/countdown
+   is running. Correct register readback establishes that the reset route
+   is unmasked. Neither alone proves that expiry resets this phone.
+2. A USB disconnect near the expected expiry is consistent with reset,
+   but cannot distinguish reset from another USB failure by itself.
+3. Confirm expiry only after observing the next boot or independent reset
+   evidence from a controlled expiry test. Confirm native `reboot` and
+   `reboot recovery` outcomes separately.
+
+As of the p34 EL3-reservation A/B attempt, countdown and ARM64 PMU unmask
+readback were verified (`fe7fffff/feffffff`). USB then disconnected about
+32 seconds after kernel entry, but the next boot had not yet been read.
+**Watchdog expiry was therefore not yet independently confirmed.** That
+attempt also used the old NX high-VA exit, so loss of its reply does not
+show that the EL3 reservation failed. A hung CPU cannot process the USB
+`reboot` command; automatic recovery depends on a separately established
+watchdog/reset path and successful USB re-enumeration. Manual recovery or
+cable replug can still be necessary.
+
+## Validation records
+
+QEMU Cortex-A53 software tests use the actual Image and loader return
+assembly. Earlier tests covered checkpoints 1..12, callee-saved
+register/SP/SCTLR restoration, SP_EL0/TPIDR_EL1, MMU-on checkpoints, and
+injected BRK faults before/after MMU and task setup. Later targeted tests
+covered return and BRK delivery with TTBR0 removed. Those tests do not
+establish correct behavior under the permanent TTBR1 permissions unless
+that transition is explicitly exercised.
+
+Real p32 hardware passed checkpoints 25, 26, and 27 after repairing the
+post-idmap return. The p34 baseline captured allocation PA `0xfffff000`,
+count 1, phase 4, followed by ESR `0x96000010` on an `ldr` from FIX_PMD
+VA `0xfffffffdfdc36000` during `paging_init`. This is a synchronous
+external abort on a **read**, not an observed memset write fault.
+Same-kernel A/B results after moving the exit to executable `.text` must
+be recorded before claiming the entire paging path passes.
+
+QEMU does not emulate this Exynos USB gadget or secure-memory protection.
+Hardware transcripts and per-attempt results are under
+`out/continue-20261016/` and `out/rootcause-20261016/`; read the named build
+and attempt rather than treating all historical logs as current firmware.
